## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. The mechanism of the FSE-based aptasensor and double stir-bars assisted target recycling system
3. Results and discussion3.1. The mechanism of the FSE-based aptasensor and double stir-bars assisted target recycling systemThe structure of the FSE-based aptasensor, consisting of double stir-bars system and NMOF labeled aptamer probes, was shown in Scheme 1. Kanamycin (KANA) was employed as model analyte to illustrate the strategy. Firstly, the signal probes were prepared by immobilizing S1 covalently (a single-stranded DNA containing KANA aptamer and toehold sequence) on the surface of NMOF-F. Meanwhile, bar-a was modified by mercapto S2 (a complementary single-stranded DNA, cDNA of aptamer in S1) and bar-b by mercapto S3 (a complementary single-stranded DNA of S1) base on self-assembly of Au-S bond. Secondly, the signal probes consisting of S1 were hybridized with S2 on bar-a. During the target recycling, the added analyte will preferentially bind with the signal probes on bar-a to form the complex based on the combining of the target with the aptamer sequence in S1. Because the aptamer has higher affinity to the target than to its cDNA, the KANA can replace the cDNA then signal probe-KANA complex will be formed and finally released from bar-a to the supernatant. The probe-KANA complex can subsequently react with S3 on bar-b to form S3-signal probe hybrid and release the target for the next cycle. This is mainly because the binding affinity between S1 and S3 (longer toehold sequence) is greater than that between S1 and KANA. Hence the double stir-bars assisted target recycles can occur and gain signal amplification. Finally, bar b with transferred signal probes was then removed to another vial containing total ionic strength adjustment buffer (TISAB) to elute \( \mathrm{F}^{-} \)for FSE measurement. The signal can be employed for quantification of the target. Notably, the procedure on removing bar-b to another vial for \( \mathrm{F}^{-} \)measurement can not only avoid matrix interference but also can prevent the interference from endogenous \( \mathrm{F}^{-} \)in the samples. As it is well known, FSE was a POCT device (Fig. S-7B) which can detect \( \mathrm{F}^{-} \)within half of a minute (Burnett et al., 2000; Lee et al., 2017). Moreover, we employed a portable double stir-bars assisted target recycling system for detection. They were shown in the following Fig. S-7A.
NMOF was successfully prepared and was suitable for adsorption \(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}^{-}\)to construct fluoride encapsulated signal probes. In addition, the BET spectrum was employed to compare the specific surface area of the UiO-66-COOH and the UiO-66-COOH-F\(^{-}\)to further verify the successful encapsulation of the NMOF-F\(^{-}\)complex. The results were showed in Fig. S-8, which showed that the specific area decreased from \(892.5\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) to \(564.2\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after UiO-66-COOH was encapsulated with \(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}^{-}\). The results meant that \(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}^{-}\)maybe intercalated into the cavities of the NMOF.
In order to increase the amount of signal probes on bar-a for target recycling, AuNPs were modified on bar-a and -b to increase their specific area. To further confirm the desired results, UV–Vis absorption spectrometry was employed to characterize the conjugation of ssDNA on AuNPs. As shown in Fig. S-3, the characteristic UV peak at \(257\,\mathrm{nm}\) of DNA was observed on the ssDNA functionalized bare gold bar and bar-a@AuNPs. Moreover, the larger decrease value of ssDNA (S2)'s UV absorption intensity (about 3.5 folds, \(400\,\mathrm{nmol}\)) was achieved on the bar-a@Au NPs than bare bar-a, which proved that the amount of S2 immobilized on the surface of the bar-a@AuNPs was more than that on bare gold bar. The aptamer hybrid efficiency of S1 with S2 on bar-a@AuNPs can also be measured by UV–Vis absorption spectrometry. The hybrid amount of S1 on bar-a@AuNPs-S2 was about \(320\,\mathrm{nmol}\). We also determined the labeling amount of S3 on bar-b@AuNPs was about
## 3.3. Optimization of the length of the DNA sequence
To verify whether the double stir-bars based toehold-mediated target recycling signal amplification reaction can be established, the DNA sequences in probes were optimized by various combinations of S1, S2, S3 and KANA. As it can be seen in Fig. 2-A, Lane 1 and 2 represent S1 \((0.2\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) with KANA aptamer sequence and S2 \((0.3\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\), respectively. Lane 3 represents the hybrid of S1 and S2 (excessive S2 hybrid with S1 to ensure the full combination). It can be seen that an obvious band representing S2/S3 hybrid chains at 65 bps formed and some S2 residues remained. Lane 4 represents the reaction between S1/ S2 and KANA \((50\,\mathrm{nM})\). The results showed that there were three bands from top to bottom in Lane 4. The band of S1/S2 at the top became
bleached, while S1 band reappeared and S2 became darker. All these demonstrated that \(50\,\mathrm{nM}\) KANA can hybrid with the S1 (with aptamer and toehold sequence) and then released S2 from S1/S2. All above results illustrated that the affinity between KANA and S1 with aptamer sequence is stronger than that of S1/S2. Moreover, the replacement of signal probes by KANA from S2/signal probe on bar-a can be realized successfully.
To further investigate the suitable toehold number on S1, different toeholds were designed due to the number of toeholds that can greatly influence the efficiency of target recycling. All the sequences involved in the optimal experiments were listed in Table S-1. S2 \((0.3\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) with different toehold numbers 1, 3, 5, 6, 7 and 8; (red chain represents the base number of aptamer sequences, while the green is the number of toehold chains) designed from lane 5–10 (Fig. 2) to react with KANA. Lane 11–16 represented the reaction between S1/KANA \((50\,\mathrm{nM})\) and S3 \((300\,\mathrm{nM})\) containing different toehold numbers. It can be found that there was no hybrid band between S1 and S3 (S1/S2) when the toehold number was 1, 3, 5, respectively, then S1 and S3 were separated. There was a light S1/S3 hybrid band shown when the toehold number was 6 (Lane 14). When the toehold number reached to 7, the color of S1/S3 band became darker (Lane 15) than that of Lane 14. And the darkness remained almost the same as Lane 15 when the toehold number reached to 8 (Lane 16). All these results meant the toehold number can obviously influence the efficiency of toehold mediated target recycling. Considering the optimal replacement efficiency and reducing the preparing cost of signal probes, the toehold length of 7 was chosen as the optimal hybrid length for subsequent experiments.
## 3.4. Optimization of experimental conditions
Some reaction conditions must be optimized, including the elution time of NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{\mathrm{(\infty)}}\) @Apt by TISAB, reaction time and temperature for double stir-bars recycling system, to gain the best measurement performance. Insufficient elution time can reduce the detection signal and affect the detection sensitivity. As shown in Fig. 3-A, the potential of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) increased as elution time increased then reached to the maximum value at \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) . The recycling time for the antibiotics to react between double stir-bars is another vital parameter to increase the signal-amplified folds of the aptasensor. Fig. 3-B displayed the potential behavior of the aptasensor in KANA \((5.0\,\mathrm{nM})\) . The potential responses increased gradually with the extended reaction time, and reached the maximum value after \(40\,\mathrm{{min}}\) Fig. 3-C showed the signal of KANA at different reaction temperatures. The height of the histogram represented the difference values between the sample and blank signal. With the temperature increasing, the difference value increased simultaneously and achieved the maximum temperature from \(25{-}40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . All these meant that room temperature can be selected for further studies. Above all, \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) of elution time and \(40\,\mathrm{{min}}\) reaction time at ambient
temperature were chosen in the following experiments.
## 3.5. Interference from endogenous \(F\)
In order to further verify whether the proposed method can avoid the interference of endogenous \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) in sample, confirmation experiments were executed. The tap water (TW), milk (Purchased in local market), urine and serum (provided from medical school of Ningbo university. The treatment of all the samples was strictly in compliance with the medical ethics of Ningbo university) were selected as typical samples due to both TW and milk containing \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) (Li et al., 2016; Massoudinejad et al., 2016). As seen in Fig. 3-D, there is no change of potential signals in the above samples. And the value was close to the fluoride-free solution \((-380\,\mathrm{mV})\) . We further designed the \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) spiked \((10\,\mathrm{mM},100\,\mathrm{mM})\) TW, milk, urine and serum samples and there were also no obvious signal changes. The reasons may be due to that no \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) can pass through the aptamer shell and \(\mathrm{F}\) ions have been saturated in the signal probes. Finally, \(100\,\mathrm{nM}\) KANA was spiked in the real samples, and the potential signals were increased dramatically to \(-354\,\mathrm{mV}\) . All these meant the endogenous \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) in samples will not interfere with the detection effect of the aptasensor.
## 3.6. The elution of \(F\) from signal probes by different reagents
One important issue for the assay is to investigate the elution conditions of the signal probe, a series of elution experiments were performed using different buffers. As shown in Fig. 4-A, the potential signal of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) in pure was basically near to blank in PBS and TISAB because there was no \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) inside. The signal \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) from NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) washed by PBS and \(150\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) (both at \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.4\)) was also the same and the signals of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) were much larger than NMOF, which meant that PBS and \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) could wash away the \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) from NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) . We found NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) was stable and leaking very few \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) in \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{HPO}_{4}\) solution existing in PBS. Because PBS also contains about \(150\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) , these mean that \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) was replaced by \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) through ion exchange (Meenakshi and Viswanathan, 2007). When NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) was labeled with aptamer to form NMOF\(\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) signal probes and washed by PBS and \(150\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) (both at \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\)), the \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) signal greatly reduced and was close to pure NMOF. All these meant that \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) in NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) cannot be washed out from them. We measured the zeta potentials of NMOF, NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) and NMOF\(\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) (Fig. S-4). The potential was \(-7.0\,\mathrm{mV}\) for NMOF, \(-13.5\,\mathrm{mV}\) for NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) and \(-17.4\,\mathrm{mV}\) for NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) . The lower potential of NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) signal probes than NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) and NMOF meant there were more negative charges on it. The reason was because large amounts of aptamer with negative charges were modified on NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) . As a result, \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) is not easily passed through the aptamer shell into the NMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{F}^{-}\) core component to replace \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) . That is why the signal probes can remain stable in PBS and the encapsulated \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) can't be washed by
PBS or \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\). All these enabled the reactions done in PBS. However, the \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) signal was the highest from NMOF- \(\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) washed by TISAB. We also measured that the zeta potential of NMOF- \(\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) was \(-12.5\,\mathrm{mV}\) at TISAB \((\mathrm{pH}\,5.0)\) because the negative charges of aptamer decreased with \(\mathrm{pH}\). These ensured the \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) (TISAB contains \(58\,\mathrm{mg\,mL}^{-1}\) of \(\mathrm{NaCl.}\)) in TISAB can pass through the aptamer layer (shell) to replace \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\). We calculated the elution amount of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) from NMOF- \(\mathrm{F}^{-}@\mathrm{Apt}\) was \(41.5\,\mathrm{mg\,g}^{-1}\) which was the same as NMOF- \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) \((42\,\mathrm{mg\,g}^{-1})\). These results demonstrated that all \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) can be completely washed out by TISAB for signal development. Based on these phenomena, TISAB was employed to wash \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) after the double stir-bars assisted recycling reaction, and PBS was employed for reaction.
## 3.7. The signal amplification effect of the assay
Fig. 4-B exhibited the signal amplified effect in detection system. It
can be seen that in the absence of KANA (Fig. 4-B-a), the signal is close to \(-360\,\mathrm{mv}\). In the presence of KANA \((20.0\,\mathrm{nM})\), only bar-a was used to react with KANA. The potential signal increased to \(-355\,\mathrm{mv}\) (Fig. 4-B-b). The change was very faint. However, when the double stir-bars assisted toehold-mediated target recycling was introduced to react with \(20.0\,\mathrm{nM}\) KANA (Fig. 4-B-c), the potential value decreased obviously to \(-331\,\mathrm{mv}\). From the calibration line between potential to \(\mathrm{pF}^{-}\), 8 times the amount of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) can be detected in the double stir-bars system compared with the unamplified one. The double stir-bars assisted signal amplification can significantly improve the sensitivity. Additionally, the length of the gold bar was optimized based on the reused times and the actual experimental cost. The results (Fig. S-9) showed that the gold bars with \(20\,\mathrm{cm}\) in length can reach to a plateau. After that, the sensitivity showed limited enhancement and the longer bar have not significant effect for sensitivity. Because the specificity of the sensor depends on the aptamer probes on the stirring bars, the length of gold bar
can't influence the specificity. We also measured the reusability of stir bar. The longer gold bars can provide more sites for immobilizing more aptamer probes, thus the stir bar can be reused for more times. We found the stir bar with \(20\,\mathrm{cm}\) can be reused for at least 25 times detection. This proved the stir bar with \(20\,\mathrm{cm}\) length can immobilize enough probes for detection. Therefore, according to the actual detection effects and in order to avoiding the waste of gold wires, the gold bar with \(20\,\mathrm{cm}\) was chosen in this experiment.
## 3.8. The selectivity and crosstalk evaluation
Firstly, KANA with its analogues (aminoglycoside antibiotics: STR, RIB or GENT) and their mixture were selected to validate the selectivity and specificity of the proposed sensor. As it can be seen in Fig. 5-A, the potential value of analogue antibiotics (between \(-364\) and \(-365\,\mathrm{mV}\)) were almost as same as that of blank \((-365\,\mathrm{mV})\). While the potential value of KANA \((-318\,\mathrm{mV})\) and that of the mixture (KANA mixed with other antibiotic, \(-321\,\mathrm{mV}\)) exhibited almost the same value. These results proved the analogues don't interfere with the detection of KANA while using KANA probes. Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 5-B, good selectivity and specificity of the proposed sensor were also evidenced by employing several other kinds of representative antibiotics, (tetracycline antibiotics OTC, amphenicol antibiotics CAP, macrocyclic antibiotics ETM or antibiotics PEN) and their mixture as analytes. We also testify if there was cross-talking for CAP probes using the same detection platform. Different antibiotics were selected as interfering substances, including CAP's analogues (TAP, FF) and other kinds of antibiotics (OTC, KANA, ETM, PEN). As shown in Table S-7, the potential of CAP \((-318\,\mathrm{mV})\) and the mixture (CAP with the interfering antibiotics, \(-322\,\mathrm{mV}\)) were almost same, while the signal of analogues antibiotics and other kinds of antibiotics was as same as the blank \((-365\,\mathrm{mV})\). These results indicated that this biosensing method can differentiate multiple antibiotics in a sample and have not potential crosstalk for multiple target antibiotics, including its analogues. In addition, we further investigated the potential interference from other coexisting
substances ( \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\) carbamide, trioxypurine; \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{K}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\), \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\)) to assess the specificity. The results indicated that the coexisting substances had little influence \((<5.0\%\) versus the blank signal) on the detection (Fig. 5-C). All results demonstrated that the system possessed good ability to resist matrix interference. The reasons were as the following: after the double stir-bars assisted recycling reaction, the bar b with signal probes was transferred to another pure reaction system only containing TISAB and no matrix in samples were transferred. Thus, the matrix influences were eliminated.
## 3.9. The analytical performance of the assay
The linear curve for logarithms concentrations of KANA fitted a regression equation of \(I\left(\mathrm{mv}\right)=14.00\,\log\,C_{KANA}-352.4\) (\(I\) represents the potential of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\), and \(C_{KANA}\) is the final concentration of KANA) with a correlation coefficient \(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9989\) (Fig. 5-D). The detection range for KANA was \(1.0\sim200\,\mathrm{nM}\) with detection limits of \(0.35\,\mathrm{nmol}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) (S/ \(\mathrm{N}=3\)), which was competitive or better than other reported single analyte detention assays including other electrochemical, fluorescence aptasensors (Table S-8) (Blanchaert et al., 2013; El-Attug et al., 2011; Qin et al., 2017; Ramezani et al., 2016; Sharma et al., 2017; C.S. Wang et al., 2016; X.Z. Wang et al., 2016). The good performance and high sensitivity of the aptasensor were attributed to following features the usage of sensitive FSE as detector; high adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\)in NMOF and the double stir-bars assisted toehold-mediated target recycling for amplification. Moreover, portable FSE as transducer of the aptasensor can obviously reduce the size of detection system and meet the requirement of POCT. All these made it promising for practical and convenient application in the detection of antibiotics in complex samples.
We continuously detected 10 samples (10 mL) with \(50\,\mathrm{nM}\) KANA by the aptasensor, for investigate the reproducibility of the proposed assay. The signals maintained \(90\%\) of the original one. These meant there were too many signals probes in the bars which can enable adequate response efficiency, and the reproducibility was good. Moreover,
three levels of KANA (2.0, 5.0 and \(50\,\mathrm{nM}\)) were used for five replicate measurements to evaluate the intra-day precision, and the inter-day precision of the assay. As listed in Table S-9, the relative standard deviations (RSDs) of intra-day were \(0.16\%\), \(0.26\%\) and \(0.51\%\) for three levels of KANA, respectively, which indicated an acceptable precision and reproducibility for this method. The RSDs of inter-day were \(0.18\%\), \(0.32\%\) and \(0.98\%\) for different concentration of KANA, respectively. These results showed a good repeatability. Additionally, the stability of the aptasensor was estimated after being stored at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). There was no obvious change after the aptasensor was stored and the signal decreased only \(5\%\) after 3 months. Therefore, the aptasensor exhibited a high degree of high stability for antibiotics detection.
## 3.10. Application of the aptasensor for detection of KANA in complex samples
To evaluate the analytical effects of the proposed assay in complex samples, the primary and spiked (5.0 and \(50.0\,\mathrm{nM}\) KANA) milk, fish, urine and serum samples were employed to analyze the target by the proposed method. As shown in Table 1, an acceptable accuracy of KANA detection in the four kinds of samples was obtained with the spiked recoveries from \(91.8\%\) to \(108\%\). Good precision was also obtained by assaying relative standard deviation (RSD) via experiments for three times and the RSD values ranged from \(0.23\%\) to \(1.33\%\).
## 3.11. Fabrication of aptasensor for chloramphenicol detection
In addition, we also fabricated an aptasensor to detect CAP by the same principle to further verify the general applicability of the above double stir-bars assisted target recycling and signal conversion strategy. The sequences of S1, S2 and S3 were shown in SM (Table S-2). The results exhibited the established FSE-based aptasensor can detect as low as \(0.46\,\mathrm{nM}\) or \(\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) CAP with satisfactory performance in terms of accuracy range from \(93\%\) to \(104\%\) (Table S-10, Fig. S-6), which demonstrated that the proposed assay is a universal platform for antibiotic detection.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, a novel FSE-POCT method based double stir-bars assisted toehold-mediated target recycling strategy was developed for quantification of organic small molecular analyte (such as KANA or CAP) in real samples. Using the specially designed signal probes (NMOF-F@Apt) based on nano UiO-66-COOH with encapsulated \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\), the FSE can indirectly detect organic small molecular analyte. A double stir-bar assisted toehold-mediated target recycling strategy was designed for signal amplification and matrix interference avoidance, which can gain 8 fold enhancement of sensitivity. However, the application of the FSE-POCT method should be extended in the future work. More antibiotics and other small organic molecules should be employed to verify the developed assay. Furthermore, some portable, low-cost and robust analytical strategies will be developed for other small organic molecules in complex samples.
